  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: c18dce35c4f218942974316577a84ef5, type: 3}
  m_Name: Scene08_Context
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::OhmsLaw.SceneAgentContext
  sceneId: Scene08
  sceneTitle: From Ohm's Lab to Our Lab
  askWattsonEnabled: 1
  covered:
  - 'Everything from Scenes 5 to 7: voltage as the push (V, volt), current as charge
    per second (I, ampere), Ohm''s repeated 1826 investigation and his apparatus.'
  - 'Ohm''s instruments turned into today''s: his torsion galvanometer into an ammeter,
    his thermoelectric source into a controllable DC source, his copper test wires
    into the nichrome wire XY, his mercury cups into a key; a voltmeter joined them.
    The relationship being tested is the same.'
  - An ammeter measures current (I, ampere). A voltmeter measures potential difference
    (V, volt).
  - The nichrome wire XY is the test conductor; X and Y are its two ends.
  - A variable DC source (cells) sets the potential difference; a key (switch) opens
    and closes the circuit.
  doNotSpoil:
  - How the ammeter and voltmeter are connected (series and parallel, Scene 9)
  - The pattern between V and I
  - V is proportional to I
  - V = IR
  - Resistance
  - Ohms
  - The Christmas-lights answer
  faq: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 2121abc4d0ed89b4781c41dacbc18dee, type: 2}
